---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Messages — IonPath" current="/guides/messages/">
  <div class="animate-fade-in-up">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-2">Messages</h1>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-8">Messages are the primary data structures in IonPath. They define named, indexed field collections that are serialized to CBOR for wire transmission.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-12 animate-fade-in-up-delay-1">

    <section>
      <h2 id="basic-syntax" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Basic Syntax</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Declare a message with the <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg</code> keyword:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-2">/// A registered user account.</span>
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">User</span> {'{'}
    <span class="text-accent-2">/// Stable identifier, unique per tenant.</span>
    id: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>;
    <span class="text-accent-2">/// Display name shown to other users.</span>
    name: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
    <span class="text-accent-2">/// Primary contact address.</span>
    email: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Each field has a <strong>name</strong> and a <strong>type</strong>, separated by a colon and terminated with a semicolon. The <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">///</code> lines are <a href="/guides/comments/" class="text-accent hover:underline">doc comments</a> — they attach to the declaration below them and are re-emitted as XML docs, JSDoc and rustdoc. Plain <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">//</code> comments (used for the inline notes elsewhere on this page) are discarded.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="field-indexing" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Field Indexing</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Fields are automatically assigned sequential indices starting from 0. These indices determine the <strong>wire identity</strong> of each field — they are used as CBOR array positions during serialization.</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">User</span> {'{'}
    id: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>;       <span class="text-text-muted">// index 0</span>
    name: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;   <span class="text-text-muted">// index 1</span>
    email: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;  <span class="text-text-muted">// index 2</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 flex items-start gap-3 rounded-xl border border-status-degraded/20 bg-status-degraded-subtle p-4">
        <span class="text-status-degraded text-sm mt-0.5">⚠</span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary"><strong>Reordering fields is a breaking change.</strong> The field index determines its position in the CBOR array. If you reorder fields, existing clients that read index 0 as <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">id</code> would now read a different field. Use <a href="/guides/schema-lock/" class="text-accent hover:underline">schema lock</a> to detect this.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="optional-fields" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Optional Fields</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Append <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">?</code> to make a field optional. It wraps the type in <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Maybe&lt;T&gt;</code>:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">UserProfile</span> {'{'}
    id: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>;
    name: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
    bio: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>?;       <span class="text-text-muted">// nullable — Maybe&lt;string&gt;</span>
    avatarUrl: <span class="type-keyword">uri</span>?;   <span class="text-text-muted">// nullable — Maybe&lt;Uri&gt;</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">In C# this generates <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">IonMaybe&lt;string&gt;</code>; in TypeScript, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">string | null</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="array-fields" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Collection Fields</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Append <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">[]</code> for a variable-length array, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">[N]</code> for a fixed-size one. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Set</code> have no suffix form and are written out:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">SearchResult</span> {'{'}
    query: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
    results: <span class="type-name">User</span>[];              <span class="text-text-muted">// array of User messages</span>
    scores: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>[];                  <span class="text-text-muted">// array of floats</span>
    embedding: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>[<span class="text-accent-2">768</span>];          <span class="text-text-muted">// exactly 768 floats</span>
    facets: <span class="type-name">Map</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">string</span>, <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>&gt;;    <span class="text-text-muted">// keyed counts</span>
    seenIds: <span class="type-name">Set</span>&lt;<span class="type-keyword">u8</span>&gt;;           <span class="text-text-muted">// distinct ids</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">See <a href="/guides/collections/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Collections</a> for the wire formats, the canonical ordering both sorted forms use, and which types may be a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Map</code> key.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="with-clause" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Reusing Fields with <code class="font-mono">with</code></h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A <a href="/guides/mixins/" class="text-accent hover:underline"><code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">mixin</code></a> is a reusable field set. Naming one in a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> clause splices its fields into the message at compile time:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">mixin</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'}
    createdAt: <span class="type-keyword">datetime</span>;
    createdBy: <span class="type-keyword">guid</span>;
{'}'}

<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Document</span> <span class="text-accent-3">with</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'}
    title: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
{'}'}
<span class="text-text-muted">// Document has three fields, in this order: createdAt, createdBy, title</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3"><strong>Mixin fields come first</strong>, in <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> order, then the message's own. That is the field <em>numbering</em>, so it is a hard contract — see <a href="/guides/mixins/#order" class="text-accent hover:underline">Field order is the contract</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="inline-types" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Inline Anonymous Types</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A field's type can be an anonymous <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg {'{'} … {'}'}</code> written in place. The compiler names it <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">{'{'}Owner{'}'}{'{'}PascalCasedFieldName{'}'}</code> and lifts it to a top level declaration:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Order</span> {'{'}
    shipping: <span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> {'{'} address: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>; {'}'};   <span class="text-text-muted">// becomes msg OrderShipping</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">The derived name is real — it goes into <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">ion.lock.json</code> and into the generated code — so a collision with an explicit declaration is an error rather than a silent rename. See <a href="/guides/inline-types/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Inline Types</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="partial-fields" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Partial Fields</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">The <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">~</code> <strong>suffix</strong> turns a message type into a sparse patch over that message, where only the fields you touch are serialized:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">UserService</span>() {'{'}
    UpdateUser(id: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>, patch: <span class="type-name">User</span>~): <span class="type-name">User</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Like <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">?</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">[]</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">~</code> is written <em>after</em> the type name. In C# this generates <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">IonPartial&lt;User&gt;</code>. Unlike every other Ion type, a partial is encoded as a CBOR <em>map</em> rather than a positional array — see the <a href="/guides/partial/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Partial&lt;T&gt;</a> guide.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="nested-types" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Referencing Other Messages</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Messages can reference other messages as field types:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Address</span> {'{'}
    street: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
    city: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
    zip: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
{'}'}

<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Company</span> {'{'}
    name: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
    address: <span class="type-name">Address</span>;           <span class="text-text-muted">// nested message</span>
    branches: <span class="type-name">Address</span>[];         <span class="text-text-muted">// array of nested messages</span>
    headquarters: <span class="type-name">Address</span>?;      <span class="text-text-muted">// optional nested message</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Forward references are supported — you can reference a message that is declared later in the file or in a different module (imported with <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">#use</code>).</p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Recursive messages</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3">A message may reference itself, directly or through a chain of other messages, <strong>as long as the recursion can terminate on the wire</strong>. The compiler tests this with one rule:</p>
      <div class="glass rounded-xl p-4 mb-4 border-l-2 border-accent/40">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-1">A reference cycle is an error only if every edge on it is <em>unconditionally owned</em>.</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary mt-2">A bare <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">x: T;</code> is owned: every value of the owner contains one, always. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T?</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T[]</code>, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">T~</code> and a union arm are <em>cycle-breaking</em>, because each can terminate — absent, empty, omitted from the patch, or a different arm.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3">So a tree is expressible:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">TreeNode</span> {'{'}
    label: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
    children: <span class="type-name">TreeNode</span>[];    <span class="text-text-muted">// breaking — a leaf has none</span>
    parent: <span class="type-name">TreeNode</span>?;       <span class="text-text-muted">// breaking — the root has none</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">And so is a mutually recursive graph, which is the case that used to be rejected outright:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Folder</span> {'{'} items: <span class="type-name">Item</span>[]; {'}'}
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Item</span>   {'{'} parent: <span class="type-name">Folder</span>?; {'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">What is <em>not</em> allowed is a loop you cannot get out of. A bare self-reference is the smallest example, and it is now caught — it used to be skipped silently, producing a type no encoder can finish writing:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">A</span> {'{'} a: <span class="type-name">A</span>; {'}'}           <span class="text-text-muted">// ION0030</span>
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">B</span> {'{'} c: <span class="type-name">C</span>; {'}'}           <span class="text-text-muted">// ION0030 — B → C → B, both edges owned</span>
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">C</span> {'{'} b: <span class="type-name">B</span>; {'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Making any one edge on the loop breaking fixes it. See <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0030" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0030</a> for the full rule and the reasoning.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="wire-format" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Wire Format</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Messages are encoded as <strong>CBOR arrays</strong> with a fixed length. Each element at index <em>i</em> corresponds to the field at index <em>i</em>:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-text-muted">// Ion</span>
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Point</span> {'{'} x: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>; y: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>; {'}'}

<span class="text-text-muted">// CBOR wire representation (conceptual)</span>
[1.5, 2.7]   <span class="text-text-muted">// index 0 = x, index 1 = y</span></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="generated-code" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Generated Code</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3">A message like:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Vector</span> {'{'} x: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>; y: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>; z: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>; {'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3 mb-3">Generates in C#:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">public sealed class</span> <span class="type-name">Vector</span>
{'{'}
    <span class="text-accent-3">public</span> <span class="type-keyword">float</span> x {'{'} <span class="text-accent-3">get</span>; <span class="text-accent-3">set</span>; {'}'}
    <span class="text-accent-3">public</span> <span class="type-keyword">float</span> y {'{'} <span class="text-accent-3">get</span>; <span class="text-accent-3">set</span>; {'}'}
    <span class="text-accent-3">public</span> <span class="type-keyword">float</span> z {'{'} <span class="text-accent-3">get</span>; <span class="text-accent-3">set</span>; {'}'}
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3 mb-3">And in TypeScript:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">export interface</span> <span class="type-name">Vector</span> {'{'}
    x: <span class="type-keyword">number</span>;
    y: <span class="type-keyword">number</span>;
    z: <span class="type-keyword">number</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</DocsLayout>
